::ENG2_U04_UA_Q01::
Question ID: ENG2_U04_UA_Q01
[MLA.ENG2.RS.01] Source set: A student is researching whether pre-order lunch forms can reduce unwanted trays. Which research question is most focused for this inquiry?{
~A. What is the history of every school lunch program?#Incorrect. This is too broad for the inquiry.
=B. How can student pre-order lunch forms help reduce unwanted trays?#Correct. This question is specific, researchable, and aligned to the unit inquiry.
~C. Why do all students like different foods?#Incorrect. This is broad and not focused on unwanted trays.
~D. Are forms always good or always bad?#Incorrect. This is absolute and not research-focused.
}



::ENG2_U04_UA_Q02::
Question ID: ENG2_U04_UA_Q02
[MLA.ENG2.RS.01] Source: A district pilot report includes before-and-after tray data from a pre-order lunch program. Why is this source useful?{
~A. It has a formal title.#Incorrect. A title alone does not prove usefulness.
~B. It is long.#Incorrect. Length does not prove relevance or reliability.
~C. It mentions school in general.#Incorrect. General school information is not enough.
=D. It provides evidence directly related to the research question.#Correct. The source gives data about the exact topic being researched.
}



::ENG2_U04_UA_Q03::
Question ID: ENG2_U04_UA_Q03
[MLA.ENG2.R.10] Original: "Reminders helped more students complete the pre-order form after the first week." Which paraphrase is accurate?{
=A. After the first week, reminders increased student completion of the pre-order form.#Correct. This accurately restates the source in new wording.
~B. Reminders made the form unnecessary.#Incorrect. This changes the meaning.
~C. Students completed no forms after reminders.#Incorrect. This contradicts the source.
~D. The form was removed after one week.#Incorrect. This is not stated.
}



::ENG2_U04_UA_Q04::
Question ID: ENG2_U04_UA_Q04
[MLA.ENG2.RS.01] Which note best records source information without copying too much?{
~A. "The report says the thing was good."#Incorrect. This is vague.
~B. "All waste ended forever."#Incorrect. This overstates the source.
=C. Pilot report: fewer unwanted trays after meals were prepared from student selections.#Correct. This note is concise and accurate.
~D. "Lunch exists."#Incorrect. This is not useful research information.
}



::ENG2_U04_UA_Q05::
Question ID: ENG2_U04_UA_Q05
[MLA.ENG2.W.03] Which sentence best integrates source evidence?{
=A. A district pilot report found fewer unwanted trays after staff prepared meals from student pre-order selections.#Correct. This clearly identifies the source and finding.
~B. The report said stuff happened.#Incorrect. This is vague.
~C. Trays and forms were a thing.#Incorrect. This is informal and unclear.
~D. Everyone knows forms are good.#Incorrect. This is unsupported.
}



::ENG2_U04_UA_Q06::
Question ID: ENG2_U04_UA_Q06
[MLA.ENG2.W.03] Which sentence best explains why the evidence matters?{
~A. Evidence is always evidence.#Incorrect. This does not explain this evidence.
~B. A source has information.#Incorrect. This is too general.
~C. Reports are written by people.#Incorrect. This does not explain relevance.
=D. The finding matters because student selections helped staff prepare meals students were more likely to choose.#Correct. This explains the connection between evidence and purpose.
}



::ENG2_U04_UA_Q07::
Question ID: ENG2_U04_UA_Q07
[MLA.ENG2.W.04] Which revision best fits a student audience?{
~A. Pre-order compliance creates operational efficiency.#Incorrect. This is too formal for students.
=B. Completing the form helps the cafeteria prepare meals students are more likely to choose.#Correct. This is clear and audience-aware.
~C. The thing helps lunch stuff.#Incorrect. This is vague and informal.
~D. Food systems must optimize outputs.#Incorrect. This is unclear.
}



::ENG2_U04_UA_Q08::
Question ID: ENG2_U04_UA_Q08
[MLA.ENG2.C.03] Which visual best supports the research finding?{
~A. A random hallway photo.#Incorrect. It does not connect to the evidence.
~B. A decorative background only.#Incorrect. Decoration alone does not explain evidence.
=C. A labeled before-and-after chart showing unwanted trays before and after the pilot.#Correct. This visual directly supports the source finding.
~D. A picture of unrelated school supplies.#Incorrect. This visual is unrelated.
}



::ENG2_U04_UA_Q09::
Question ID: ENG2_U04_UA_Q09
[MLA.ENG2.RS.01] Which source would be least useful for the research question about pre-order forms and unwanted trays?{
~A. A cafeteria manager interview about form completion.#Incorrect. This is relevant to the process.
~B. A pilot report with tray data.#Incorrect. This is directly relevant.
~C. A student feedback summary about understanding the form.#Incorrect. This is relevant to audience communication.
=D. A blog post about unrelated sports uniforms.#Correct. This source does not address lunch forms or unwanted trays.
}



::ENG2_U04_UA_Q10::
Question ID: ENG2_U04_UA_Q10
[MLA.ENG2.W.03] Which transition best adds a related source finding?{
=A. In addition#Correct. This transition adds related information.
~B. However#Incorrect. This usually signals contrast.
~C. Instead#Incorrect. This signals replacement.
~D. Although#Incorrect. This introduces contrast or qualification.
}



::ENG2_U04_UA_Q11::
Question ID: ENG2_U04_UA_Q11
[MLA.ENG2.L.01] Which sentence is complete and clear?{
~A. Because the source shows fewer trays.#Incorrect. This is a fragment.
~B. Source trays fewer students because.#Incorrect. This is unclear.
=C. The source shows fewer unwanted trays after the pre-order pilot.#Correct. This is complete and clear.
~D. When the form and chart.#Incorrect. This is incomplete.
}



::ENG2_U04_UA_Q12::
Question ID: ENG2_U04_UA_Q12
[MLA.ENG2.C.01] Which presentation plan best shows collaboration?{
~A. One person speaks while the team ignores the source.#Incorrect. This does not show shared, source-based work.
=B. One member explains the evidence, one explains the synthesis, one explains the visual, and one explains the revision choices.#Correct. This plan shows purposeful collaboration.
~C. Everyone repeats the same unsupported opinion.#Incorrect. This is repetitive and not evidence-based.
~D. The team skips the visual explanation.#Incorrect. The audience needs the publication choices explained.
}



::ENG2_U04_UA_Q13::
Question ID: ENG2_U04_UA_Q13
[MLA.ENG2.RS.01] Which synthesis best combines Source A and Source B? Source A: The pilot report found fewer unwanted trays. Source B: Reminders helped more students complete the form.{
=A. The pilot reduced unwanted trays, and reminders supported the process by helping more students complete the form.#Correct. This accurately combines both source ideas.
~B. The sources are both about school.#Incorrect. This is too general.
~C. Reminders proved the report was false.#Incorrect. This does not match the source information.
~D. The pilot had no connection to forms.#Incorrect. This contradicts the source set.
}



::ENG2_U04_UA_Q14::
Question ID: ENG2_U04_UA_Q14
[MLA.ENG2.W.04] Which sentence should be revised because it is not audience-aware?{
~A. Completing the form helps staff prepare meals students are more likely to choose.#Incorrect. This is student-friendly.
~B. The chart shows fewer unwanted trays after the pilot.#Incorrect. This is clear.
~C. A reminder can help students complete the form on time.#Incorrect. This is clear and relevant.
=D. Stakeholders must enact compliance with food-service optimization protocols.#Correct. This wording is too formal and unclear for students.
}



::ENG2_U04_UA_Q15::
Question ID: ENG2_U04_UA_Q15
[MLA.ENG2.C.03] Which publication choice best matches Unit 4 standards?{
~A. A copied report with no audience revision.#Incorrect. The product should be adapted for its audience.
~B. A poster with no source evidence.#Incorrect. The product must be source-based.
=C. A student-facing flyer with accurate evidence, clear explanation, and a labeled visual.#Correct. This matches source-based writing and collaborative multimedia publication.
~D. A private note with no publication plan.#Incorrect. The unit includes publication.
}



::ENG2_U04_UA_Q16::
Question ID: ENG2_U04_UA_Q16
[MLA.ENG2.W.03] Which sentence overstates the evidence?{
~A. The pilot report found fewer unwanted trays.#Incorrect. This is measured and accurate.
=B. Pre-order forms will eliminate all cafeteria waste forever.#Correct. This claims more than the evidence proves.
~C. Reminders helped more students complete the form.#Incorrect. This accurately states the source finding.
~D. A chart can help students understand the change.#Incorrect. This is reasonable.
}



::ENG2_U04_UA_Q17::
Question ID: ENG2_U04_UA_Q17
[MLA.ENG2.R.10] Which paraphrase keeps the meaning of this source detail: "Students understood the purpose better when the message used simple wording and a labeled chart"?{
~A. Students did not need any message.#Incorrect. This contradicts the source detail.
~B. A chart made all problems disappear.#Incorrect. This overstates the source.
=C. Simple wording and a labeled chart helped students understand why the form mattered.#Correct. This restates the meaning accurately.
~D. Students only cared about colors.#Incorrect. This is not stated.
}



::ENG2_U04_UA_Q18::
Question ID: ENG2_U04_UA_Q18
[MLA.ENG2.W.03] Which sentence has the most appropriate academic voice?{
=A. The pre-order process helped staff prepare meals based on student selections.#Correct. This is precise and evidence-based.
~B. The lunch form was super awesome.#Incorrect. This is informal and exaggerated.
~C. The report said stuff.#Incorrect. This is vague.
~D. Students should do the thing.#Incorrect. This is vague and informal.
}



::ENG2_U04_UA_Q19::
Question ID: ENG2_U04_UA_Q19
[MLA.ENG2.C.03] Chart data: Before the lunch pre-order pilot, students left 42 unwanted trays. After the pilot, students left 18 unwanted trays. Which caption best supports this before-and-after chart?{
~A. This chart is colorful.#Incorrect. This does not explain the evidence.
=B. The chart shows unwanted trays decreased from 42 before the pilot to 18 after the pilot.#Correct. This caption explains the before-and-after data shown in the chart.
~C. A chart appears here.#Incorrect. This is too vague.
~D. Charts are useful sometimes.#Incorrect. This is general and unsupported.
}



::ENG2_U04_UA_Q20::
Question ID: ENG2_U04_UA_Q20
[MLA.ENG2.L.01] Which revision fixes the fragment: "Although some students needed reminders."{
~A. Although some students needed reminders and.#Incorrect. This is still incomplete.
~B. Some although students reminders.#Incorrect. This is unclear.
~C. Although reminders.#Incorrect. This is still a fragment.
=D. Although some students needed reminders, form completion improved after the first week.#Correct. This creates a complete sentence.
}



::ENG2_U04_UA_Q21::
Question ID: ENG2_U04_UA_Q21
[MLA.ENG2.RS.01] Which detail best supports the idea that communication affects form completion?{
=A. Student feedback said simple wording and a labeled chart helped students understand the form's purpose.#Correct. This source detail connects communication choices to student understanding.
~B. The cafeteria has tables.#Incorrect. This does not address communication or form completion.
~C. The school has hallways.#Incorrect. This is unrelated.
~D. Students take several classes.#Incorrect. This does not support the idea.
}



::ENG2_U04_UA_Q22::
Question ID: ENG2_U04_UA_Q22
[MLA.ENG2.W.04] Which question should guide final revision?{
~A. Can I make the wording harder so it sounds advanced?#Incorrect. Revision should improve clarity.
~B. Can I remove the evidence to shorten the product?#Incorrect. The communication must remain source-based.
=C. Will the intended audience understand the evidence, explanation, visual, and action step?#Correct. This checks audience awareness and publication readiness.
~D. Can I use a random visual to fill space?#Incorrect. Visuals should support meaning.
}



::ENG2_U04_UA_Q23::
Question ID: ENG2_U04_UA_Q23
[MLA.ENG2.C.01] Which oral explanation best supports the publication plan?{
~A. We picked the chart because it looked nice.#Incorrect. This does not explain how the visual supports evidence.
~B. We did not use the sources.#Incorrect. The publication must be source-based.
~C. We made the words complicated so adults would notice.#Incorrect. The audience is students.
=D. We used a labeled chart because it shows the change in unwanted trays and helps students understand the evidence quickly.#Correct. This connects visual choice, evidence, and audience needs.
}



::ENG2_U04_UA_Q24::
Question ID: ENG2_U04_UA_Q24
[MLA.ENG2.W.03] Which sentence best combines evidence, explanation, and audience purpose?{
~A. The report is about lunch and students should care.#Incorrect. This is vague.
=B. The pilot report found fewer unwanted trays after staff used student selections, so students should complete the form to help the cafeteria prepare meals they are more likely to choose.#Correct. This integrates evidence, explains why it matters, and gives an audience-focused action.
~C. Lunch forms are a thing at school.#Incorrect. This is too general.
~D. The chart is nice and the form exists.#Incorrect. This does not explain the evidence.
}



::ENG2_U04_UA_Q25::
Question ID: ENG2_U04_UA_Q25
[MLA.ENG2.C.03] Which final checklist best represents the Unit 4 workflow?{
=A. Research question, reliable source, accurate paraphrase, synthesis, expository explanation, audience revision, and purposeful visual.#Correct. This checklist follows the full research-to-publication process.
~B. Random topic, copied source, no explanation, unrelated visual.#Incorrect. This does not match Unit 4 standards.
~C. Opinion only, no source check, no revision.#Incorrect. Unit 4 requires source-based communication.
~D. Difficult words, hidden evidence, and decoration only.#Incorrect. Clarity, evidence, and audience fit are required.
}



::ENG2_U04_UA_Q26::
Question ID: ENG2_U04_UA_Q26
[MLA.ENG2.RS.01] Topic: school gardens. Which research question has the best scope for one assignment?{
~A. Are gardens good?#Incorrect. This is too broad and mostly opinion-based.
~B. What is a plant?#Incorrect. This is too narrow and asks for a simple definition.
=C. How could a school garden support science learning for tenth-grade students during one semester?#Correct. This question is focused by topic, audience, subject area, and time frame.
~D. Why should everyone love gardens forever?#Incorrect. This wording is biased and too broad.
}



::ENG2_U04_UA_Q27::
Question ID: ENG2_U04_UA_Q27
[MLA.ENG2.RS.01] A student asks, "What about recycling?" What is the main problem with this research question?{
~A. It is too narrow because it can be answered with one fact.#Incorrect. The problem is not that it is too narrow.
=B. It is only a vague topic, not a focused research question.#Correct. It does not ask what the student wants to learn about recycling.
~C. It is already fully refined.#Incorrect. It needs a focus, audience, place, or effect.
~D. It includes too much evidence.#Incorrect. It includes no evidence.
}



::ENG2_U04_UA_Q28::
Question ID: ENG2_U04_UA_Q28
[MLA.ENG2.RS.01] Broad topic: cafeteria food waste. Which revision narrows the topic by solution and time frame?{
=A. How could a pre-order lunch form reduce cafeteria food waste during one semester?#Correct. This question names a solution and time frame.
~B. Is food waste bad?#Incorrect. This is broad and opinion-based.
~C. What is food?#Incorrect. This is too narrow and asks for a definition.
~D. Why is lunch the best class?#Incorrect. This is not a researchable academic question.
}



::ENG2_U04_UA_Q29::
Question ID: ENG2_U04_UA_Q29
[MLA.ENG2.RS.01] Research question: How could a school garden support science learning? Source A: A state university agriculture extension article by a science education specialist explains classroom garden observations and was updated in 2025. Why is Source A reliable?{
~A. It gives no author or evidence.#Incorrect. The source includes an author role and relevant examples.
~B. It is an advertisement for tools.#Incorrect. The source is a university extension article, not an ad.
~C. It uses insults instead of evidence.#Incorrect. No loaded insult is described.
=D. It is current, relevant, and written by a knowledgeable specialist.#Correct. These features support reliability and credibility.
}



::ENG2_U04_UA_Q30::
Question ID: ENG2_U04_UA_Q30
[MLA.ENG2.RS.01] Source B says, "Only foolish schools avoid gardens." What does this wording show?{
=A. Loaded language that suggests bias.#Correct. "Only foolish schools" attacks one side instead of providing evidence.
~B. Neutral academic tone.#Incorrect. The wording is not neutral.
~C. Current data from a study.#Incorrect. The sentence gives no data.
~D. A valid measurement method.#Incorrect. No method is described.
}



::ENG2_U04_UA_Q31::
Question ID: ENG2_U04_UA_Q31
[MLA.ENG2.RS.01] Research question: How could a school garden support science learning? Source A says gardens let students observe plant growth, soil conditions, and ecosystem relationships. Which note is most useful?{
~A. Gardens are outside.#Incorrect. This is true but too general to answer the research question.
~B. Students are people.#Incorrect. This does not help answer the research question.
=C. Gardens can support observations of plant growth, soil, and ecosystems.#Correct. This note is brief, accurate, and relevant to the research question.
~D. The source has words.#Incorrect. This is not useful source information.
}



::ENG2_U04_UA_Q32::
Question ID: ENG2_U04_UA_Q32
[MLA.ENG2.R.10] Original: Garden journals can help students record observations and connect evidence to science vocabulary. Which paraphrase is accurate?{
~A. Journals make science unnecessary.#Incorrect. The original says journals support science work.
~B. Garden journals are always perfect.#Incorrect. The original does not make that absolute claim.
~C. Students should never write observations.#Incorrect. The original says journals help students record observations.
=D. Students can use garden journals to track what they see and explain it with science terms.#Correct. This keeps the meaning in new wording.
}



::ENG2_U04_UA_Q33::
Question ID: ENG2_U04_UA_Q33
[MLA.ENG2.W.03] Findings: A pre-order lunch pilot reduced unwanted trays. Student reminders helped more students complete forms. Which organization pattern fits best?{
~A. Chronological story plot#Incorrect. The goal is to explain research findings, not narrate a story.
=B. Cause and effect#Correct. The paragraph should explain how the form and reminders led to reduced waste.
~C. Random list#Incorrect. Research findings need logical organization.
~D. Definition only#Incorrect. The findings explain an effect, not only a definition.
}



::ENG2_U04_UA_Q34::
Question ID: ENG2_U04_UA_Q34
[MLA.ENG2.W.03] Which sentence uses the best academic voice?{
~A. Lunch forms are super awesome and fixed everything.#Incorrect. This is informal and exaggerated.
~B. Everybody should just do lunch forms because I said so.#Incorrect. This is informal and unsupported.
=C. Pre-order lunch forms may reduce waste when staff prepare meals from student selections.#Correct. This is clear, measured, and source-based.
~D. Forms are, like, the best thing ever.#Incorrect. This is informal and biased.
}



::ENG2_U04_UA_Q35::
Question ID: ENG2_U04_UA_Q35
[MLA.ENG2.W.03] Finding: A district report found that the pre-order pilot reduced unwanted trays. Which sentence best integrates the source?{
=A. A district report found that the pre-order pilot reduced unwanted trays after staff prepared meals from student selections.#Correct. This sentence identifies the source and states the finding clearly.
~B. The report had stuff about trays.#Incorrect. This is vague and informal.
~C. Trays went down or whatever.#Incorrect. This is informal and unclear.
~D. The lunch thing was awesome.#Incorrect. This is opinion, not source integration.
}



::ENG2_U04_UA_Q36::
Question ID: ENG2_U04_UA_Q36
[MLA.ENG2.W.03] Which transition best adds a related second source?{
~A. However#Incorrect. This usually signals contrast.
~B. Instead#Incorrect. This signals replacement or contrast.
~C. Although#Incorrect. This introduces contrast or qualification.
=D. In addition#Correct. This transition adds related information.
}



::ENG2_U04_UA_Q37::
Question ID: ENG2_U04_UA_Q37
[MLA.ENG2.C.03] A student team is creating a one-page digital flyer for students about reducing unwanted lunch trays. Which choice best matches the audience and purpose?{
~A. Copy every paragraph from the report onto the flyer.#Incorrect. A flyer for students needs selected information, not an entire report.
=B. Use a short headline, one clear research finding, and a simple visual that shows why completing the pre-order form matters.#Correct. This choice combines audience awareness, evidence, and multimedia design.
~C. Use only a decorative image with no explanation.#Incorrect. A visual must support the message and evidence.
~D. Write a long formal memo to cafeteria staff.#Incorrect. The assigned audience is students, not cafeteria staff.
}



::ENG2_U04_UA_Q38::
Question ID: ENG2_U04_UA_Q38
[MLA.ENG2.W.04] Draft text for students: "Failure to comply with pre-order procedures results in inefficient meal allocation." Which revision is most audience-aware?{
=A. Completing the pre-order form helps staff prepare meals students are more likely to choose.#Correct. This keeps the meaning accurate while making the message clearer for students.
~B. Students are failing the lunch system.#Incorrect. This blames the audience and does not improve clarity.
~C. Lunch allocation inefficiency exists.#Incorrect. This is still vague and too abstract.
~D. Forms are forms, so do them.#Incorrect. This is informal and does not explain the purpose.
}



::ENG2_U04_UA_Q39::
Question ID: ENG2_U04_UA_Q39
[MLA.ENG2.W.03] Source finding: A district pilot report found fewer unwanted trays after staff prepared meals from student pre-order selections. Which sentence is most publication-ready?{
~A. The lunch thing helped stuff.#Incorrect. This is vague and informal.
~B. Forms fixed every waste problem forever.#Incorrect. This overstates the evidence.
~C. Students should just do the form.#Incorrect. This gives an action but does not explain the evidence.
=D. The pilot report found fewer unwanted trays after staff prepared meals from student pre-order selections.#Correct. This sentence is accurate, specific, and source-based.
}



::ENG2_U04_UA_Q40::
Question ID: ENG2_U04_UA_Q40
[MLA.ENG2.C.03] Which visual best supports the source-based message?{
~A. A random cafeteria photo with no label.#Incorrect. A visual must support the evidence.
~B. A decorative border around the title.#Incorrect. Decoration does not explain evidence.
=C. A labeled before-and-after chart showing unwanted trays before and after the pilot.#Correct. This visual directly helps the audience understand the source finding.
~D. A school mascot image with no connection to lunch forms.#Incorrect. This visual is unrelated to the message.
}
